= ψ Resonator =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = A ψ resonator is a bounded region with Neumann boundary conditions that stores ψ field energy in discrete standing-wave modes at quantised frequencies — it is the ψ equivalent of an LC tank circuit, a microwave cavity, or an optical Fabry-Pérot, and the [[Psi Stabilizer]] shell is a ψ resonator specifically engineered to store energy in the ψ₀₁₀ mode whose field maximum falls at the operator's temporal lobes.
| everyday_analogy   = A church bell rings at a specific frequency because its shape confines acoustic waves to certain standing patterns and forbids others. Change the shape, change the frequency. A ψ resonator does the same thing for ψ waves: the shell geometry determines which ψ frequencies are supported inside and which cannot exist, creating a frequency-selective storage element that accumulates ψ energy at precisely the operating frequency while rejecting everything else.
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A discrete mode spectrum <math>\omega_{nlm}</math> determined entirely by the shell geometry, effective mass <math>m_{\rm eff}</math>, and boundary condition type — no free parameters once these are fixed; (2) A stored energy that builds up toward a steady-state value with time constant <math>\tau = 2Q_\psi/\omega</math> after the drive is engaged; (3) A spatial mode shape <math>\psi_{010}(\mathbf{x})</math> with its maximum at the equatorial plane of the oblate spheroid, corresponding anatomically to the temporal lobes; (4) A ringdown time of at least 1.6 ms at Q = 200, enabling measurement of ψ field persistence after the drive ceases.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The ψ resonator does not just contain the ψ field — it selects which field pattern can exist inside it. The mode shape, not just the drive frequency, determines which anatomical region experiences maximum ψ exposure. The [[Mode Geometry Test]] (F3) is designed precisely to isolate this mode-shape effect experimentally.
}}

== Mathematical Origin ==

The ψ resonator emerges directly from the bounded wave equation. Inside a closed region <math>V</math> with Neumann boundary conditions <math>\partial\psi/\partial n = 0</math> on the surface <math>\partial V</math>, the time-harmonic Helmholtz equation:

:<math>\left(\nabla^2 + k^2\right)\psi_{nlm}(\mathbf{x}) = 0, \qquad k^2 = \omega^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2</math>

admits solutions only at discrete eigenvalues <math>k_{nlm}</math> — the resonant wave numbers. The corresponding resonant frequencies:

:<math>\boxed{\omega_{nlm}^2 = m_{\rm eff}^2 + k_{nlm}^2}</math>

The eigenvalues <math>k_{nlm}</math> depend entirely on the geometry of <math>V</math> and the boundary condition type. For the oblate spheroidal geometry of the [[Psi Stabilizer]] with Neumann conditions, they are computed numerically (COMSOL RF module eigenvalue solve).

