== ψ Implementation ==

=== Geometry: The Oblate Spheroidal Shell ===

The [[Psi Stabilizer]] ψ resonator is an oblate spheroid:
* Semi-axes: <math>a = 110</math> mm (equatorial), <math>b = 90</math> mm (polar), matching 95th-percentile adult head geometry
* Eccentricity: <math>e = \sqrt{1-b^2/a^2} \approx 0.57</math>
* Aspect ratio: <math>a/b \approx 1.22</math>

The oblate spheroidal geometry is not merely anatomically convenient — it is geometrically selected. The ψ₀₁₀ lowest Neumann mode of an oblate spheroid places its field maximum at the equatorial plane. As the shell is fitted over the head, this plane aligns with the temporal lobes. A spherical shell would place the lowest mode maximum at the same amplitude everywhere on the equatorial great circle — anatomically less targeted. A prolate spheroid would tilt the mode maximum toward the poles (frontal/occipital lobes). The oblate spheroid is the geometry that selects temporal lobe targeting from among the available modes.

=== Material: TPMS + Composite Fill ===

The resonator wall is a [[TPMS Lattice Design]] (B7) Gyroid shell at density parameter <math>t = 0.4</math> (≈ 30% relative density), filled with the [[Smart Composite Fill]] (B8). The effective medium parameters at the Stabilizer design point:

* <math>\varepsilon_\psi \approx 4.0\,\varepsilon_0</math> (BST composite at 0V bias)
* <math>\mu_\psi \approx 1.04\,\mu_0</math> (ferrofluid at H = 200 A/m)
* <math>m_{\rm eff} \approx 5</math> m⁻¹ (giving <math>L_\psi = 20</math> cm)
* <math>\tan\delta_\psi < 0.005</math> (optimised formulation from B4)
* <math>Q_\psi > 200</math> (material-limited)

The wave speed inside the shell: <math>v_\psi = c/\sqrt{\varepsilon_\psi\mu_\psi} \approx c/2</math>. The resonant wave number for ψ₀₁₀ at 40 kHz:

:<math>k_{010} = \sqrt{\omega_{010}^2/v_\psi^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2} \approx \sqrt{(2\pi\times4\times10^4)^2/(c/2)^2 - 25} \approx 1.68\,\text{m}^{-1}</math>

This wave number is consistent with the oblate spheroidal geometry at the target dimensions — verified by COMSOL.

=== Drive Coupling: Bifilar Coil ===

The bifilar coil from [[Bifilar Coil Engineering]] (D1) drives the resonator via the <math>\alpha F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}</math> coupling term. Coupling efficiency:

:<math>\eta_{\rm coup} = \frac{|\int \psi_{010}(\mathbf{x})\,\alpha E^2_{\rm bifilar}(\mathbf{x})\,d^3x|^2}{\int|\psi_{010}|^2\,d^3x \cdot \int|\alpha E^2_{\rm bifilar}|^2\,d^3x}</math>

This overlap integral is maximised when the bifilar coil E-field distribution spatially matches the ψ₀₁₀ mode shape. The coil geometry is optimised to this criterion — the coil winding pattern is not uniform but follows the mode amplitude contours.

